\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\f0\fs24\cf0
\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Every artist who has ever tried to capture the beauty of nature has failed. Why they even try is a mystery. They don\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t seem to understand that beauty is out there in the world, and it\u8217\'92s connected to everything else. To truly capture it, you\u8217\'92d have to cut it out, and that would kill it. Not only is that impossible to do, it\u8217\'92s disrespectful to even try. The hunt for beauty in art is insane.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri burped.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took another swig of the burgeon hooch that he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d left steeping in a jar. This jar was hidden in a hole halfway between Vigram\u8217\'92s rich leech farm, and his own failed bean farm. He had a few hidey holes like this, spread out along the way. He\u8217\'92d sip one hole\u8217\'92s hooch, and by the time he was finished, his legs had carried him to the next. He replenished each stop with a little bit of water, sugar, and the burgeons he\u8217\'92d bought from Geem. This burgeon hooch was far from tasty, but it was alright enough to make the long, long walk tolerable. Already, the sun was dipping.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took another swig. He took a longer swig, then continued spiraling over the fact that he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d ever picked up a paintbrush.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Because what was a painting, really? Paint a tree, and all those leaves get reduced to flecks of green. A living leaf with all its complexity, its pattern, its anastomosing veins pumped full of vital fluids, its green birth and brown death, its fall from the branch, its decay into dirt, that whole nested universe, reduced to a fleck of green. And this gets to the truth that no artist wants to admit: art has nothing to do with the truth. It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s about seduction. Art is about \u8216\'91look at }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 this}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 , and please ignore }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 that\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\af1\uc1\u8217\'92}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 . Paint an oasis, and look, there\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a naked woman bathing in the mossy water. Focus the viewer\u8217\'92s gaze by sharpening the droplets of jade that gleam on her cinnamon skin. The leaf? Feather its edge into the sky. Blame it on the effects of atmospheric haze. Better yet, don\u8217\'92t paint the leaf, paint bunches of leafy shapes. Think about removing the tree entirely, because it\u8217\'92s good practice to erase all evidence of a crime after it\u8217\'92s been committed.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The seduction runs deep into every fiber of the artist\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s being. Seduced by themselves, they see the wonder of creation and think, \u8220\'93People ought to know about }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 my}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  interpretation.\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 As if creation was not already speaking. And this is true: every painting, every poem, they\u8217\'92re all cutting god off mid-sentence. There\u8217\'92s proof, here, of the futility of human existence. Stand outside a public art gallery and watch the crowds of enablers herd themselves indoors to admire the manic displays of self-absorption. The complexity of reality is right outside, but they\u8217\'92d all rather see it cornered, killed, flayed, then put on display, with choice sections of its hide stitched into pretty frames. The world is easier to digest that way. Makes one feel less like an idiot.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Go and paint all this,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, sweeping his arm over the sunset. \u8220\'93It\u8217\'92s not possible. Take as long as you like. Take a lifetime. Take ten lifetimes! Then\u8230\'85 and then\u8230\'85\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And then he forgot what he was on about because the ugliest spot of land in all the world was spread out before him. This was his farm. Still, the point remained, because this ugliness, too, was impossible to capture in an image.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 His dusty farm was impaled with so many unproductive bean poles that it looked a bit like a gentle hill had picked a fight with a porcupine. Some of those poles were wrapped with withered beanstalks, looking like martyred pilgrims. And there shallow pits all over, divots, each stained with an irregular ring of salt.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri ambled up to his broken swing gate, feeling lost. He took a massive swig of his burgeon hooch, as the hooch made those salt stains come alive. Even now, they wiggled and sparkled in the red light of the sun. They gnawed on his wilted bean stalks, making it seem like he hadn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t lost a battle to an inanimate thing. That salt was a fiery, pestilential swarm, sent by demons.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He sighed.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t so bad. The high from these down-valley burgeons made textures slide and edges bleed. The chief effect was that anything bright would sparkle\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 the salt, the rims of the mountainous puffs of cloud overhead, the splayed rays of the dying sun, pretty much everything that wasn\u8217\'92t plunged into shadow would sparkle. The effect was less pronounced when the burgeon was mixed in with a nasty hooch like this, but it was alright. Even in this fading light, his short hornscrub tree looked beautiful. The tree\u8217\'92s gray bark sparkled wherever the sunlight filtered through its drifting boughs. If he were to paint, he would paint that tree. He would never do that, because it would be impossible to capture the movement of the light. That was another thing a painting could never capture\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 the image of one moment bleeding into another moment.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Something moved on the top of his hill. High up, far out, silhouetted against the warm pastels of the sky, an unusual dark blur lumbered around his bean poles.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri tried to will his mind back into sobriety so that he could properly see what it was. Some sort of animal.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Would you look at that,\u8221\'94 he mused.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  It was an ox. It was the same damned yellow-headed ox from Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s leech farm.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 What were the chances?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 This far out?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mruuuuu,\u8221\'94 the ox said when it noticed him gawking. It sauntered through burgeon addled haze, dewlap flapping. The skip in its step suggested it recognized him. And as it came into focus, the state of its distress became all too clear: red scrapes all over, courtesy of the thorns surrounding Vigram\u8217\'92s farm. Purple bruises splotched its armpits. The rest of its shaven body was a grotesque mosaic of little blood rings where leeches used to be. Those leeches still attached were concentrated on its haunches, with a few suckling on its belly.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The poor thing was obviously unable to care for itself, and so it was coming to him for help. It had no idea where it was. There wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t any food around for miles. Absent a miracle, it was going to die.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s heart went out to the creature.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I can\u8217\'92t do anything for you,\u8221\'94 he said, but even as he said it, he knew it wasn\u8217\'92t true. He could do something. He could scrape off those leeches. He could lead the beast to water. He could go to the extreme and devote the rest of his meaningless life to caring for that ox. He wasn\u8217\'92t going to do that, but he could! He could. Should he? The answer was an obvious no, because this was only an ox. Thousands of oxes just like it were being butchered and eaten throughout the world. He\u8217\'92d eaten plenty of beef himself. Helping this ox and not the others would make him a hypocrite. There was a whole leech farm full of animals that needed saving down the road, and he hadn\u8217\'92t spared one thought for helping any of them. Were they somehow less worthy than this ox? Was he somehow responsible for the life of every living being in all creation? Where was the line?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Technically, he did own this ox. The purchase involved a tacit of assumption of responsibility. But, he hadn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t actually expected to ever see the beast again. Why was it here? How? It had wandered off Vigram\u8217\'92s farm, and just so happened to find his?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He suspected foul play of some sort. Fate was never so neat. Or, maybe the ox itself was intelligent. Some say newborn gods can take the form of animals.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He cast a skeptical eye upon the ox as it picked its path around the crooked bean poles. It was clearly too dumb to be a god. And too weak. Poor thing, the whites of its eyes were red from being forced to sniff all that freeweed smoke. When it came to a stop just feet from him, the fluid inside the fat sun blisters on its back continued to swish around.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Damn,\u8221\'94 Mandri muttered.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The beast really needed help.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 But, so did he. That was the way of the world. Creatures had to take care themselves. There just wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t enough concern to go around.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri sipped his hooch. He held his hand up to bless the ox. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I release you from my service,\u8221\'94 he said in regal tones.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took another sip. The burgeonsight had the short yellow hairs on the ox\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s head swirling. He sipped his hooch again and downed the dregs. He had more hooch sitting inside his hornscrub tree and wanted it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Stop looking at me like that,\u8221\'94 he said. \u8220\'93I can\u8217\'92t do anything for you.\u8221\'94 Of course, he could do something, but he\u8217\'92d said what he said, and it could not be taken back.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He made for his tree.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The damned ox followed him.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He sat down in the tree\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s shade, which by now, was the world\u8217\'92s shade, and the ox did too\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it lay on its belly like it hadn\u8217\'92t done in over a week. It\u8217\'92s heavy ribs squished a few leeches.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Do we know each other?\u8221\'94 Mandri asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The ox stared back at him, full of bovine dumbness. Its big, dozing breaths rolled its bleeding hide back and forth over its sagging spine. Now that Mandri was positively high on burgeons, the sight of that rolling hide was a sight to see. The skin appeared to boil.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He nestled deeper into the tree trunk\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s embrace as the nightly breeze caressed his cheek. He sighed a little half-sipped, absent sigh, the kind of sigh that he\u8217\'92d make whenever he was ready for a nap. He let his eyelids slouch as he watched flies buzz in and out of ox\u8217\'92s snout. The ox snorted, blowing those fuzzy black dots out of his great big nostrils. The flies zipped right back in to lick at the only reliable source of moisture that they could find in this godforsaken field.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri let his eyes shut. The swish of the wind brushing the leaves lulled him to sleep.}}